# An autologin root shell on the console.
#
# WHY busybox getty AND NOT agetty. agetty is util-linux, which is not in this
# set and is not planned for it. busybox is in the sysroot, statically linked,
# and already PID 1's shell in the initramfs -- so this adds no package.
#
# `-n` skips the login prompt. There is no password database worth
# authenticating against yet: /etc/passwd names root with an `x` and there is
# no /etc/shadow, so a real login would fail closed and leave an unusable
# console. Autologin is the honest state of a system that has no user
# management, and saying so here is better than shipping a prompt nobody can
# satisfy.
#
# THE TTY IS NOT NAMED HERE ANY MORE. It was, and the name was wrong on two of
# the three places this system boots: ttyAMA0 is the ARM PL011 UART, x86_64
# under qemu needs ttyS0, and real hardware has no serial port and needs tty1.
# getty cannot open a device that is not there, exits 1, and `restart = true`
# loops forever -- correct behaviour, wrong argument.
#
# scripts/console-getty reads `console=` from /proc/cmdline, which is the
# kernel's own answer to the same question, and execs getty on it. See that
# file. This service now says WHAT to run and not WHERE.
#
# `-l` NAMES A WRAPPER, NOT /bin/sh, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. getty
# execs the -l program with argv[0] set to the path given, so /bin/sh started
# as a NON-login shell and /etc/profile was never sourced -- measured on the
# real kernel as PATH=/sbin:/usr/sbin:/bin:/usr/bin, which is busybox's
# built-in default and the REVERSE of the one /etc/profile sets. See
# scripts/console-shell for why that ordering matters.
# run-as = veron, LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE A PERSON TOUCHES.
#
# This was the last thing in the session running as uid 0, and it was the
# largest hole in moving the desktop off root: a shell on a VT with no
# authentication in front of it is root for anyone who presses Ctrl+Alt+F2.
# There is no su, no sudo and no doas in this image, and no /etc/shadow for
# busybox su to consult, so the shell being unprivileged is the whole of the
# boundary.
#
# THE RECOVERY PATH IS DELIBERATE AND IS NOT HERE. A machine that needs root
# gets it from GRUB with `rdinit=/bin/busybox sh`, which requires physical
# access and a reboot. Anything privileged the running system needs is a dinit
# service in this directory -- root-owned, unwritable by veron, and startable
# through /run/dinitctl by group power.
#
# IT NEEDS THE TTY TO BE OPENABLE. devtmpfs creates /dev/tty* as root:tty
# 0620, which a non-root getty cannot use; the device-nodes service regroups
# the one this will land on. If that service did not run, this fails to start
# and says so rather than silently falling back to root.
type = process
command = /etc/dinit.d/scripts/console-getty
run-as = veron
restart = true
depends-on = early-filesystems
depends-on = device-nodes
# STRAIGHT TO THE SERIAL CONSOLE, BECAUSE A FILE NEEDS A READER AND THE
# READER IS DEAD. These services die silently at boot in the CI guest; the
# /run logfile captured their words and the tmpfs discarded them, and the
# fixture that was added to cat the file typed into a console that had
# already rate-limited to death -- qemu echoed the keystrokes back and
# nothing executed them. /dev/console needs no shell: the dying process
# speaks directly into the boot log the harness already captures.
logfile = /dev/console
